{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "Dashboard Ingestion API: Auth, Strict Deserialize, Validate, Store",
  "description": "The consumer side of the boundary. Follow an inbound aggregate batch through the dashboard's minimal API: endpoint wiring, API-key authentication with a constant-time hash comparison, strict JSON deserialization that rejects unknown fields, defense-in-depth server-side validation (including repository/PII checks), and an idempotent Table Storage upsert keyed by the org from the *key record* — never the payload. Seventh in the series.",
  "nextTour": "Dashboard Analytics: From Table Buckets to Radzen Charts",
  "steps": [
    {
      "file": "src/dashboard/AgentObservability.Dashboard/Program.cs",
      "line": 106,
      "title": "Wiring the ingestion endpoints",
      "description": "**Situation.** The Blazor host also exposes a small ingestion API for the extension to POST to.\n\n**Mechanism.** `app.MapIngestionApi()` registers the `/api/ingest/*` routes (aggregate, context-insights, status, health) as minimal-API handlers.\n\n**Implication.** Ingestion is a thin, isolated surface bolted onto the same app that serves the dashboard UI — one deployment, two responsibilities.\n\n**Gotcha.** These routes are the *only* inbound entry for extension data; everything the dashboard later charts flowed through here first."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/dashboard/AgentObservability.Dashboard/Program.cs",
      "line": 39,
      "title": "Table Storage vs. in-memory",
      "description": "**Situation.** The dashboard needs a store for ingested aggregates, but should also run locally/in tests without cloud dependencies.\n\n**Mechanism.** `ingestionTableConfigured` (line 39) checks whether an Azure Table connection is set; if so (line 43) it registers the Table-backed stores and a `ConfigApiKeyStore` (line 50), otherwise it falls back to in-memory implementations.\n\n**Implication.** The same endpoints and validation run in both modes — only the persistence swaps — so tests exercise the real pipeline.\n\n**Gotcha.** On startup the Table stores call `EnsureTablesExistAsync()` (line 80) so a fresh storage account is provisioned lazily rather than failing the first request."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/dashboard/AgentObservability.Dashboard/Services/Ingestion/IngestionEndpoints.cs",
      "line": 29,
      "title": "The aggregate route",
      "description": "**Situation.** The extension's `POST /api/ingest/aggregate` (Tour 6) lands here.\n\n**Mechanism.** `app.MapPost(\"/api/ingest/aggregate\", ...)` receives the request, and after an `options.Value.Enabled` service-disabled check (line 37), authenticates before reading the body.\n\n**Implication.** Authentication happens *before* the body is parsed, so an unauthenticated caller never reaches deserialization or validation.\n\n**Gotcha.** The handler delegates to a host-independent `HandleAggregateAsync` (called at line 47) so the exact same logic is unit-testable without spinning up Kestrel."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/dashboard/AgentObservability.Dashboard/Services/Ingestion/IngestionEndpoints.cs",
      "line": 42,
      "title": "Authenticate first",
      "description": "**Situation.** Every ingestion route must prove the caller holds a valid org API key.\n\n**Mechanism.** `authenticator.AuthenticateAsync(request.Headers.Authorization, ...)` parses the `Authorization` header and resolves it to an `AuthResult` (authenticated + org id, or not).\n\n**Implication.** The result — crucially the *org id* — is derived from the validated key, and it's what scopes storage later (never a value from the payload).\n\n**Gotcha.** The same call guards all three ingest routes (aggregate/context-insights/status), so auth semantics can't drift between endpoints."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/dashboard/AgentObservability.Dashboard/Services/Ingestion/IngestionAuthenticator.cs",
      "line": 86,
      "title": "Resolving the key",
      "description": "**Situation.** The presented token must be matched to a stored key record without leaking timing information.\n\n**Mechanism.** `AuthenticateAsync` parses the token into a key id + secret, looks up the record via `FindByKeyIdAsync` (line 104), and compares the presented secret's hash to the stored hash.\n\n**Implication.** Keys are stored as *hashes*, never plaintext, so a store compromise doesn't hand over usable secrets.\n\n**Gotcha.** When the key id is unknown, the code still performs a dummy comparison (line 110) so the response time doesn't reveal whether the id existed — a timing-oracle defense."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/dashboard/AgentObservability.Dashboard/Services/Ingestion/IngestionAuthenticator.cs",
      "line": 79,
      "title": "Peppered secret hashing",
      "description": "**Situation.** The stored hash must resist offline attack even if the table leaks.\n\n**Mechanism.** `ComputeSecretHashHex(pepper, secret)` hashes the secret together with a server-side pepper, producing the hex digest compared during auth.\n\n**Implication.** The pepper (a server secret, separate from the DB) means a stolen table of hashes still can't be brute-forced without also stealing the pepper.\n\n**Gotcha.** Both sides — issuance (the `New-IngestionApiKey.ps1` script under `infra/`) and verification — must use the same pepper, or every key silently fails to authenticate."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/dashboard/AgentObservability.Dashboard/Services/Ingestion/IngestionAuthenticator.cs",
      "line": 115,
      "title": "Constant-time comparison",
      "description": "**Situation.** A naive string `==` on the hash would short-circuit on the first differing byte, leaking secret bytes via timing.\n\n**Mechanism.** `FixedTimeEqualsHex(presentedHashHex, record.SecretHashHex)` (helper at line 159) compares the two hex hashes in constant time regardless of where they differ.\n\n**Implication.** An attacker can't iteratively recover the correct hash by measuring response latency — a classic OWASP crypto-failure defense.\n\n**Gotcha.** Constant-time comparison matters only because the compared value is secret-derived; keep any future secret comparisons on this helper rather than `==`."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/dashboard/AgentObservability.Dashboard/Services/Ingestion/IngestionEndpoints.cs",
      "line": 109,
      "title": "Reject the unauthenticated",
      "description": "**Situation.** With an `AuthResult` in hand, the handler must refuse anything that didn't authenticate.\n\n**Mechanism.** `if (!auth.IsAuthenticated) return Unauthorized();` is the first line of `HandleAggregateAsync`, before any parsing.\n\n**Implication.** Body parsing and validation are unreachable for bad callers, minimizing the attack surface exposed to unauthenticated input.\n\n**Gotcha.** Because this is inside the testable core (not the route lambda), auth-rejection behavior is covered by unit tests, not just integration ones."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/dashboard/AgentObservability.Dashboard/Services/Ingestion/IngestionEndpoints.cs",
      "line": 23,
      "title": "Strict deserialization options",
      "description": "**Situation.** The wire schema is `additionalProperties: false`; the server must honor that, not silently ignore extra fields.\n\n**Mechanism.** `DisallowOptions` sets `UnmappedMemberHandling = JsonUnmappedMemberHandling.Disallow`, so any field not on the model throws during deserialization.\n\n**Implication.** An attempt to smuggle an unexpected (possibly raw-content) field is rejected at parse time — the server mirrors the extension's strictness.\n\n**Gotcha.** This is why the model classes must exactly match the schema; adding a property to the DTO without the schema (or vice versa) breaks compatibility loudly, which is the intent."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/dashboard/AgentObservability.Dashboard/Services/Ingestion/IngestionEndpoints.cs",
      "line": 117,
      "title": "Parse, or 400",
      "description": "**Situation.** The raw body must become a typed `AggregateBatch`, rejecting anything malformed or unexpected.\n\n**Mechanism.** `JsonSerializer.Deserialize<AggregateBatch>(body, DisallowOptions)` runs inside a try/catch; a `JsonException` (including an unknown field) is turned into a `400 Bad Request` problem response.\n\n**Implication.** Unknown-field rejection is a *hard* failure here, so the store never even sees an off-contract payload.\n\n**Gotcha.** A `null` deserialization result (empty body) is also treated as a 400 — the code doesn't assume a successful parse yields a non-null batch."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/dashboard/AgentObservability.Dashboard/Services/Ingestion/AggregateBatchValidator.cs",
      "line": 58,
      "title": "Re-validate on the server",
      "description": "**Situation.** The client already validated, but the server must never trust the client.\n\n**Mechanism.** `Validate(batch)` re-checks every privacy/shape rule server-side — operation allow-list (line 142), latency-bounds match, and per-bucket constraints — returning a list of errors.\n\n**Implication.** This is defense in depth: even a compromised or buggy client can't push data that violates the contract, because the server independently enforces it.\n\n**Gotcha.** Any errors become a `ValidationProblem` (400); the batch is stored only when the error list is empty."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/dashboard/AgentObservability.Dashboard/Services/Ingestion/AggregateBatchValidator.cs",
      "line": 227,
      "title": "Repository/PII hardening",
      "description": "**Situation.** `repository` is the one free-ish string in the contract, so it gets extra scrutiny to keep PII/emails out.\n\n**Mechanism.** The validator rejects a repository containing `'@'` (line 227) and requires it to match `RepositoryRegex()` (line 236, a compiled source-generated regex), constraining it to an `owner/name`-style shape.\n\n**Implication.** An email address or arbitrary path can't ride in through the repository field — it's structurally filtered at ingest.\n\n**Gotcha.** This mirrors the extension's own repository discipline (Tour 6); the two-sided check means neither end alone is the last line of defense."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/dashboard/AgentObservability.Dashboard/Services/Ingestion/IngestionEndpoints.cs",
      "line": 146,
      "title": "Store under the authenticated org",
      "description": "**Situation.** Multi-tenant isolation depends on writing data to the correct org partition.\n\n**Mechanism.** `store.UpsertBucketsAsync(auth.OrgId!, batch, ...)` uses the org id from the *validated key record*, with an inline comment stressing: orgId is NEVER from the payload.\n\n**Implication.** A caller cannot write into another tenant's data by putting a different org in the body — the tenant is fixed by whose key authenticated.\n\n**Gotcha.** On success it returns `{ accepted, batchId }` (line 148); the count reflects buckets stored, giving the client a concrete acknowledgment."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/dashboard/AgentObservability.Dashboard/Services/Ingestion/TableAggregateStore.cs",
      "line": 37,
      "title": "Idempotent upsert",
      "description": "**Situation.** The client retries on transient failure (Tour 6's `sendWithRetry`), so the same batch may arrive twice.\n\n**Mechanism.** Rows are written with `TableUpdateMode.Replace` into the `IngestionAggregates` table (name at line 13), so a re-sent batch replaces the prior row value rather than duplicating it.\n\n**Implication.** Retries are safe — duplicate deliveries converge to the same stored state, keeping analytics counts correct.\n\n**Gotcha.** Idempotency relies on stable row keys derived from the batch's dimensions; the analytics layer (Tour 8) reads these same rows back via `QueryBucketsAsync` (line 46)."
    },
    {
      "title": "Ingestion recap",
      "description": "The server treats the client as untrusted at every step: **wire** the routes (`MapIngestionApi`) → **authenticate** with a peppered, hashed key compared in **constant time** → **strictly deserialize** (unknown fields → 400) → **re-validate** the privacy contract server-side (operation allow-list, **repository/PII** hardening) → **upsert** idempotently under the org from the *key*, never the payload.\n\nThe data now sits in Table Storage. Next, see how it's read back and turned into charts. Continue to **Dashboard Analytics: From Table Buckets to Radzen Charts**."
    }
  ]
}
